' rwin32_system_info.rr: a little look at your Windows computer through the
' Windows API itself, with RWin32.inc — RapidR's one standard set of Windows
' API declarations.
'
'   rapidr run rwin32_system_info.rr
'
' One line is all it takes: $INCLUDE "RWin32.inc" (below). It is built into
' RapidR, so there is no file to find or copy. Your program gets exactly the
' declarations it uses — here GetTickCount, GetSystemMetrics,
' GetComputerNameA, GlobalMemoryStatusEx and GetSystemInfo, with the TYPEs
' they need — and a name you declare yourself always stays yours.
'
' If you have an older program with its own WIN32API.INC, it keeps working
' as it is. Should you like to try RWin32.inc there, change its include line
' to this one; `rapidr import-rapidq --rwin32` (or RapidR Studio's import)
' does it in a copy and lists every difference first. The manual's page
' "One standard for the Windows API" tells the whole story.
'
' The structures are laid out as Windows lays them out, on 64-bit and on
' 32-bit Windows alike: MEMORYSTATUSEX has 64-bit numbers, and SYSTEM_INFO
' has pointers, which are 8 bytes wide on 64-bit Windows and 4 on 32-bit —
' RWin32.inc picks the right layout for the computer you build for.
'
' Windows only: Windows' own functions run on Windows. Elsewhere this
' program still compiles, says so kindly, and stops before the first call.

$APPTYPE CONSOLE
$INCLUDE "RWin32.inc"

DIM name AS STRING
DIM size AS LONG
DIM memory AS MEMORYSTATUSEX
DIM info AS SYSTEM_INFO

IF ENVIRON$("OS") <> "Windows_NT" THEN
    PRINT "This example talks to Windows itself, so it runs on Windows."
    PRINT "Here it compiled fine: RWin32.inc's declarations are ready when you are."
    END
END IF

PRINT "Windows has been running for "; GetTickCount \ 60000; " minutes"
PRINT "Screen: "; GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN); " x "; GetSystemMetrics(SM_CYSCREEN); " pixels, "; GetSystemMetrics(SM_CMONITORS); " monitor(s)"

name = SPACE$(MAX_COMPUTERNAME_LENGTH + 1)
size = LEN(name)
IF GetComputerNameA(name, size) THEN PRINT "Computer name: "; LEFT$(name, size)

memory.dwLength = SIZEOF(memory)
IF GlobalMemoryStatusEx(memory) THEN
    PRINT "Memory: "; memory.ullTotalPhys \ 1048576; " MB in all, "; memory.dwMemoryLoad; "% in use"
END IF

GetSystemInfo info
PRINT "Processors: "; info.dwNumberOfProcessors; ", page size "; info.dwPageSize; " bytes"
PRINT "SYSTEM_INFO is "; SIZEOF(info); " bytes in this program (48 on 64-bit Windows, 36 on 32-bit)"
